+### Importing a save on the Mac
 
-Restore is available only in a packaged app supervised by the current launcher. A second app instance using the same save folder is refused. Direct developer runs of `dmhost` must be stopped before restoring that save from an app.
+**File → Import Save…** accepts a complete native Switch save folder, a ZIP export, a complete single-file save, or an nxnative `.dmsync` backup. The source is copied into private staging and checked before the app asks to close the game. Cancel leaves the game running and its save unchanged; importing never changes the source.
+
+- Close the source game or emulator first so it cannot change files during copying. In Lichii/Eden or another emulator, use **Open Save Data Location** / **Open User Save Directory**, then select the folder containing the game's actual files. For a console export, select one game's primary-save backup from [JKSV](https://github.com/J-D-K/JKSV#features) or [Checkpoint](https://github.com/BernardoGiordano/Checkpoint#working-path), not an entire user, NAND, game collection or backup history. Recognized JKSV account-save backups are supported; device-save backups are refused rather than guessing whether the game keeps them in the primary or a separate device folder. Use a complete nxnative `.dmsync` backup to restore device-save state.
+- Folder and ZIP imports open macOS's folder browser at the root of the checked private copy. Inspect its files and choose the folder whose **contents** should become the game's save; the rest of its nested layout is preserved. Browse into a subfolder if the export wraps the actual save files. No folder is guessed or stripped automatically, so legitimate slot folders stay intact. You can import an ordinary wrapped ZIP without extracting or repacking it first.
+- At the selected root, JKSV's `.nx_save_meta.bin` is checked when it starts with JKSV's magic: the supported record is 86 bytes, revision 1, for the target game's account save. Wrong-game or unsupported records are refused. Valid exporter metadata is omitted from the imported save, not removed from the source. Same-named ordinary files without that magic, directories and nested files are left intact.
+- A single-file import keeps that file's name. Only `.zip` and `.dmsync` suffixes request container decoding; other files remain opaque even if they contain ZIP data. To keep a game's own `.zip` file intact, choose its containing folder. A single file must be the complete save, not one part of a multi-file save: use the complete folder or ZIP for those.
+- These three raw inputs **replace the primary save as a whole**, rather than merging into it. A separate current device save is preserved, including anything committed while the import was being prepared. Outside recognized JKSV metadata, nxnative cannot identify a raw save's game; metadata does not establish region, version or DLC compatibility. Native payloads remain opaque: no console-image decryption, checksum repair, other-system save conversion or emulator save-state conversion. Confirm the target game before importing.
+- ZIPs must be unencrypted. Unsafe paths or guest-name metadata, links, special files, duplicate or case/Unicode-conflicting paths, empty saves and damaged recognized archives are refused. Existing nxnative guest-name aliases are preserved, but raw case-only names are not converted into aliases. Imports are bounded to 16 GiB of expanded data, 100,000 entries (including implicit folders) and 64 path components; `.dmsync` inputs also have a 17-GiB copy limit including metadata. File names and nested folders are not translated into game-specific conventions.
+- Raw imports receive import-time file dates, not the export's old dates, so cloud conflict prompts do not present the new import as an old console backup. Full `.dmsync` backups retain their recorded file dates.
+
+`.dmsync` backups use the same checked format as cloud saves. They include committed normal and device saves and their guest-name metadata; importing one validates its title ID and replaces both saves, removing an old device save if the backup has none. Export leaves an existing archive intact if it fails.
+
+Import is available only in a packaged app supervised by the matching current launcher; a host-only update leaves it disabled for safety. After the game exits cleanly, the launcher keeps the previous save in a sibling `save.before-restore-…` recovery folder and replaces the selected save directories. It cancels replacement if the current save could not reach an enabled cloud folder, and sends the imported save there afterward. Start the game again after the result dialog. A second app instance using the same save folder is refused; direct developer runs of `dmhost` must be stopped before importing from an app.
 
