+### Importing native save files without an emulator-specific conversion
+
+- **Symptom:** a player has another emulator's save files or a console save export, but the Mac app accepts only nxnative `.dmsync` backups; a raw import then creates a fresh save because its files are below an export wrapper; or JKSV's exporter metadata travels with the save and a new import appears old in cloud conflict prompts.
+- **Cause:** the game owns its save format; emulators and console exporters place those files in different directories or ZIP containers. Preserving an export wrapper can put the files one level too deep, but automatically stripping every single directory can destroy a valid `slot_00/` layout. JKSV adds a metadata record that is not game data; an export's file dates describe the backup, not when the player imported it.
+- **Fix:** **File → Import Save…** validates a complete folder, ZIP, single-file save or checked `.dmsync` backup. The native macOS folder browser opens at the checked private folder/ZIP copy's root and lets the player choose which contained folder's contents to import. AppKit supplies the contents view instead of a separate directory index and custom preview. No folder is guessed or silently stripped. Folder copying streams entries; the shared serializer supplies canonical ordering. The selected snapshot is rebased by rename without another payload copy or decompression. A selected-root `.nx_save_meta.bin` with JKSV's magic must be the supported 86-byte revision-1 record for this title and account-save type; only then is it excluded. Unsupported recognized records, wrong titles and device-save records are refused, not routed by guesswork. Ordinary same-named files and nested files remain payload. Raw imports receive import-time file dates, replace only the primary save and leave the latest separate device save untouched; full `.dmsync` imports keep their bytes, recorded dates, checked title IDs and exact normal/device replacement semantics. Both use the launcher's after-exit transaction, recovery, lease and cloud coordination. Existing confinement, type, collision, resource and integrity checks remain; cancellation/failure leaves source and current save unchanged. [Input rules and limits](../playing.md#importing-a-save-on-the-mac).
+- **Found on:** [Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md#found-on-this-game), whose earlier Lichii transfer required no conversion; [Super Mario Bros. Wonder](../../dm/games/010015100B514000/README.md#mac-routes), whose practice save exposed the wrapper ambiguity. [JKSV metadata/date feedback](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/issues/2420#issuecomment-6869857583) is covered by native synthetic fixtures, not a new gameplay check. The importer adds no per-game filename rules.
+
+### Untrusted save imports exhaust staging or replace their source
+
+- **Symptom:** selecting a small crafted `.dmsync` can create vastly more directories before confirmation, or a C caller's differently spelled output can replace the original input on case/normalization-insensitive filesystems.
+- **Cause:** bundle records can imply many parent directories, outside the raw-import budgets. Canonicalizing an output's parent does not prove its final basename differs from the opened source's filesystem object.
+- **Fix:** imports bound packed input, payload bytes, depth, declared records and unique expanded paths, including implicit directories and legacy-name expansion inherited from parent folders. A non-materializing bounded pass validates a backup and its title before extraction. Existing source/output aliases are refused by device/inode identity, not spelling alone; rejected imports keep source, live saves and existing output unchanged. See [input limits](../playing.md#importing-a-save-on-the-mac).
+- **Found on:** synthetic archive and filesystem-alias fixtures during [Super Mario Bros. Wonder's import review](../../dm/games/010015100B514000/README.md#mac-routes), not a gameplay failure.
+
+### An import file picker opens wider than the game window
+
+- **Symptom:** the Mac save-import file picker stretches across the screen, with all its instructions on one line.
+- **Cause:** a full paragraph was assigned to `NSOpenPanel.message`; the native picker sized its heading to that long line and persisted the oversized open-panel dimensions.
+- **Fix:** keep the source picker's hint to two short lines, including the reminder to close the source emulator. The staged-folder browser uses a short heading and a width-constrained, disclosed accessory for the detailed compatibility and recovery warning; leave browser sizing to AppKit. An installation that already cached oversized dimensions needs a one-time reset of its app-specific `NSNavPanelExpandedSizeForOpenMode` value, not a global preference reset or a change to the game window.
+- **Found on:** [Super Mario Bros. Wonder](../../dm/games/010015100B514000/README.md#found-on-this-game), reported with a screenshot of the oversized native picker.
+
